S|       1       EXT.  BERKELEY, CALIFORNIA. DAY                          1           
Y|               SUBTITLE Berkeley, California -1981-                                 
O|
N,T,A|               DISSOLVE TO ACTUAL TV NEWS FOOTAGE FROM 1981                         
O|
N|               A busy intersection near the UC Berkeley campus. A strange,          
N|               self-propelled motorized gurney whirrs into view and makes           
N,I|               its way over a pedestrian crosswalk. The passenger, MARK             
N,I|               O'BRIEN, in his early 30s, is visible only from the neck up.         
N|               The rest of him is covered by a blanket. He operates the             
N|               gurney with a mouth control and a set of mirrors positioned          
N|               around his head.                                                     
C,E|                                   NEWS REPORTER (V.O.)                             
U|                         Mark O'Brien has been going to UC                          
U|                         Berkeley since 1978. That's O'Brien                        
U|                         in the motorized gurney heading for                        
U|                         class last week.                                           
N|               The gurney continues along a leafy promenade on the campus.          
N|               Passers-by just go about their normal business.                      
O|
C,E|                                   NEWS REPORTER (V.O.)                             
U|                         He had polio when he was 6 years                           
U|                         old. The disease left his body                             
U|                         crippled but his mind remained                             
U|                         sharp and alert, and since he                              
U|                         wanted to be a writer, Mark O'Brien                        
U|                         entered Cal to major in English and                        
U|                         learn his trade.                                           
N,G|               We hear a voice reciting a verse of poetry as we follow Mark         
N|               in his contraption.                                                  
C,E|                                   MARK (V.O.)                                      
U|                         Graduation                                                 
U|                         Today I hear the crowd’s applause                          
U|                         Receive congratulations from my                            
U|                         friends                                                    
U|                         Today I ask if I’ve found a place                          
U|                         among the rest                                             
U|                         I hope you see a man upon this                             
U|                         stage                                                      
U|                         Who studied...read..wrote, and                             
U|                         passed the test                                            
U|                         In cap and gown, diploma on my                             
U|                         chair                                                      
N,T|               THE SCENE CHANGES to the interior of a large auditorium. A           
N|               graduation ceremony is in progress. Suddenly, everyone in            
N|               the hall, GRADUATES, their FAMILIES, ACADEMICS and OTHERS,           
N|               rise to their feet as Mark, in his gurney, buzzes across the         
N|               stage, a mortar board hung on one of the handles.                    
O|
O|
O|                                   Final Shooting Script             1A.            
O|
C,E|                                   NEWS REPORTER (V.O.)                             
U|                         And so, Mark O'Brien graduates from                        
U|                         Cal, one of 250 English majors to                          
U|                         receive degrees today.                                     
O|
O|
O|                                   Final Shooting Script              2.            
O|
N|               The DEAN steps forward, congratulates Mark and places a              
N|               diploma on his blanket. The gurney makes its way across the          
N|               rest of the stage to thunderous applause.                            
N,T|               THE SCENE CHANGES BACK to the campus exterior. The news              
N|               reporter talks to camera.                                            
O|
C|                                   NEWS REPORTER                                    
U|                         If this report tells us anything,                          
U|                         it is that a disability is not                             
U|                         necessarily a handicap.                                    
N,G|               In the background, Mark's family and friends are gathered            
N|               round him in a jubilant mood.                                        
O|
C,E|                                   NEWS REPORTER (CONT’D)                           
U|                         Mark O'Brien teaches us that                               
U|                         courage and perseverance overcome                          
U|                         obstacles.                                                 
U|                         With Mark O'Brien at UC Berkeley,                          
U|                         Bill Hillman, Channel Five                                 
U|                         Eyewitness News.                                           
A|               END OF NEWS FOOTAGE                                                  
O|
S|       1A      EXT. SAN FRANCISCO. NIGHT                               1A           
O|
N,G|               Classic shot of the illuminated Golden Gate Bridge.                  
O|
Y|               SUPER CAPTION: “A FEW YEARS LATER”                                   
O|
S|       2       EXT.  STREET OUTSIDE MARK’S. NIGHT                       2           
O|
N,G|               It is about 4.00 a.m. All is quiet. We follow a mean-looking         
N|               alley cat to the front of a modest, ground-floor apartment.          
N|               It pauses, then slinks round the side, onto a ledge and in           
N|               through a partially-opened window.                                   
S|       3       INT.   MARK'S PLACE. NIGHT                               3           
O|
N|               The cat comes through the window, hops onto the floor and            
N|               quickly finds a nice little plate of food scraps that has            
N|               been set out specially. As it settles down to its meal, we           
N|               become aware of a heavy and regular sound, like a ship's             
N|               pump, coming from somewhere close-by.                                
N|               In the center of the room is an object that looks like a prop        
N|               from a ‘50s sci-fi movie. A human head protrudes from one            
N|               end. The object is an iron lung, and its purpose is to keep          
N|               its occupant, Mark O'Brien, breathing. Every 4.5 seconds, the        
N|               pump mechanism creates a vacuum inside, forcing Mark's chest         
N|               to expand and suck in air. He is fast asleep.                        
O|
O|
O|                                   Final Shooting Script             2A.            
O|
C,E|                                   MARK (V.O.)                                      
U|                         Breathing                                                  
U|                         Look you                                                   
U|                         This most excellent canopy, the                            
U|                         air,                                                       
U|                         Presses down upon me                                       
U|                         At 15 pounds per square inch                               
U|                         A dense, heavy, blue-glowing ocean.                        
U|                         Teasing me with its nearness and                           
U|                         immensity.                                                 
U|                         And all I get is a thin stream of                          
U|                         it.                                                        
U|                         A finger’s width of the rope that                          
U|                         ties me to life.                                           
N|               Having now eaten its fill, the cat has a good scratch, then          
N|               wanders over to the iron lung.                                       
O|
O|
O|                                   Final Shooting Script              3.            
O|
N|               It hops up onto the small platform that supports Mark's head         
N|               and slides itself along his face, once this way, once the            
N|               other way, then jumps on top of the iron lung and walks its          
N,G|               length.  Through the portholes, we just make out the shape of        
N|               Mark's bent, undersize body. Suddenly, Mark's nose twitches.         
N|               He opens his eyes and grimaces.                                      
C|                                   MARK                                             
U|                         Shit!                                                      
N|               His face continues to contort as he tries to cope with the           
N|               terrible itching. He shakes his head violently, then stops           
N|               suddenly and closes his eyes. We hear his thoughts.                  
O|
C,E|                                   MARK (V.O.) (CONT’D)                             
U|                         Okay, just focus. Now, scratch                             
U|                         with your mind, okay, your mind,                           
U|                         scratch with your mind...                                  
N|               After a couple more nose twitches, he settles down. It seems         
N|               to have worked. In his peripheral vision, Mark can see the           
N|               cat making itself comfortable in a corner chair, one of the          
N|               only other pieces of furniture in the room. The first hints          
N|               of dawn start to appear through the curtains. They                   
N|               illuminate a large framed portrait of the Virgin Mary hanging        
N|               on the wall.  Mark acknowledges it.                                  
C,E|                                   MARK (CONT’D)                                    
U|                         Good morning.                                              
O|
N|               Sunlight streams in, making the picture look truly sacred.           
O|
