C,P|                                   FATHER BRENDAN (TO MARK)                         
U|                         Mark, I know you're watching. I’m                          
U|                         sorry, my friend, but we have to do                        
U|                         this.  Just bear with me, and I                            
U|                         think you might like it.                                   
O|
C,P|                                   FATHER BRENDAN (TO THE CONGREGATION)             
E|                                   (CONT’D)                                         
U|                         I've been branching out lately,                            
U|                         reading Native American stories                            
U|                         about the character of the                                 
U|                         trickster. Sometimes he shows up                           
U|                         as a coyote, sometimes as a raven,                         
U|                         but he always does the unexpected.                         
U|                         The trickster breaks the rules.                            
U|                         Mark O'Brien, whose life we're                             
U|                         celebrating today, was very much                           
U|                         the trickster -- anyone who knew                           
U|                         him knew that.                                             
O|                                   (MORE)                                           
O|
O|
O|                                   Final Shooting Script            90B.            
O|
C,P|                                   FATHER BRENDAN (TO THE CONGREGATION)             
U|                         Besides his irreverent humor and                           
U|                         alarming honesty, he always did the                        
U|                         unexpected.                                                
U|                         In nearly every aspect of his life,                        
U|                         Mark did the unexpected. His was a                         
U|                         dynamic voice in a paralyzed body,                         
U|                         a full life lived long after he                            
U|                         should have been dead. He                                  
U|                         graduated from college, wrote and                          
U|                         published articles and poems, and --                       
U|                         against all odds, by his own                               
U|                         admission -- entered into the fully                        
U|                         human experience of physical love.                         
U|                         In this way, Mark lived from day to                        
U|                         day to day, from breath to breath                          
U|                         to breath, for 49 years. He loved,                         
U|                         and he was loved and in his quiet                          
U|                         voice, he spoke loud truths.                               
O|
O|
O|                                   Final Shooting Script             91.            
O|
N,G|               We see Rod, Vera and Laura the therapist in various rows.            
N|               Also Amanda, holding a toddler in her arms and sitting next          
N|               to a tall, boring-looking HUSBAND.                                   
C,E|                                   FATHER BRENDAN (V.O.) (CONT’D)                   
U|                         Susan Fernbach will now read one of                        
U|                         Mark's favorite poems.                                     
O|
C,E|                                   SUSAN (V.O.)                                     
U|                         This is called Love Poem for No One                        
U|                         in Particular.                                             
N,G|               We see SUSAN at the lectern, reading from a book.                    
O|
C|                                   SUSAN                                            
U|                         Let me touch you with my words.                            
U|                         For my hands lie limp as empty                             
U|                         gloves.                                                    
U|                         Let my words stroke your hair,                             
U|                         Slide down your back and tickle                            
U|                         your belly.                                                
U|                         For my hands, light and free-flying                        
U|                         as bricks,                                                 
U|                         Ignore my wishes and stubbornly                            
U|                         refuse to carry out my quietest                            
U|                         desires.                                                   
N,G|               We see Cheryl, as deeply touched by the words as she was the         
N|               first time.                                                          
C,E|                                   SUSAN (V.O.) (CONT’D)                            
U|                         Let my words enter your mind,                              
U|                         bearing torches.                                           
U|                         Admit them willingly into your                             
U|                         being                                                      
U|                         So they may caress you gently                              
U|                         within.                                                    
